  "worked_solution": "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=-4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=-1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) & =(-1)(z-4\\text{i})(z-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-(z-4\\text{i})(z+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(z-4\\text{i})(z+4\\text{i}) & =-({z}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & =-({z}^{2}-16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =-({z}^{2}+16) \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=-{z}^{2}-16.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
